{{Short description|Football tournament group stage}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=June 2018}}
'''Group H of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]]''' began on 16 June and ended on 25 June 2010.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Match Schedule 2010 FIFA World Cup South Africa |website=FIFA.com|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/64/42/24/fifawc2010matchschedulefinalversion.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091228213032/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/64/42/24/fifawc2010matchschedulefinalversion.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 December 2009 |accessdate=6 December 2009 }}</ref> The group consisted of [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]], [[Honduras national football team|Honduras]], [[Chile national football team|Chile]] and reigning [[UEFA European Football Championship|European]] (and eventual) champions [[Spain national football team|Spain]]. 

Chile and Spain were in the same group in [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]], in a group from which only the Spanish team qualified for the next round. Chile and Switzerland were also in the same group in [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], when Chile was host and went on to finish in third place. Switzerland and Spain competed in the same group in [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966]], but neither advanced to the next round. Finally, Honduras and Spain were in the same group in [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982]]. Four years later, in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup|next World Cup]], Chile and Spain would meet again in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group B|Group B]], against the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] and [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]]; Chile would upset Spain 2–0 in their second match, eliminating Spain from the tournament.

